# NET-5 (NET-REMOTE): remote smoothing - per-pawn interpolation buffer drawn an ADAPTIVE delay behind the estimated
# server clock (two snapshot intervals + the arrivals' p95 jitter, floor 100 ms, cap 350 ms; the render clock slews by
# at most 10 % time dilation, Overwatch-style), cubic Hermite between snapshots with the replicated velocity as
# tangents, extrapolation past the newest snapshot capped at 250 ms, teleports cut (replicated teleport count).
# Replicated locomotion (velocity, movement mode) drives the remote pawn's AnimGraph locally (Speed / IsFalling of
# Humanoid_Locomotion's blend space), never replicated poses (UE simulated proxy).
# Runner: <scratch>/NET-REMOTE/run_all.sh (base NET-3 run_all.sh). Server sends with 75 ms lag + 30 ms jitter
# (--lag-ms/--jitter-ms), clients send with 75 ms: 150 ms round trip. Pawn = Prefabs/Pawn_Character.deprefab
# (engine Humanoid skinned mesh + Humanoid_Locomotion graph).
# Дано: DesertServer --role all on WP_Protocol + Editor client B (observer, player 1, --shot-player-view) + probe A
#       (DesertNetProbe --bot, player 2) from task/NET-REMOTE; 150 ms + 30 ms jitter.
# Когда: A walks a circle (r 477 cm, 300 cm/s, --dir 0,0,-0.5 --turn-deg-s 36) in front of B for 20 s.
# Тогда [net5_remote_walk] net5/strip.png: B's consecutive sequence frames show A's skinned character on a smooth
#       arc, facing its walk, walk cycle matching speed (no foot skating, no jumps between frames); B's log
#       "interpolation buffer: delay ... arrival jitter p95 ..." settles at 100-200 ms (not at the 350 cap), p95 jitter
#       60-100 ms (GNS jitter avg 30 is an exponential mean, p95 ~90), 0 snap(s). A is the Humanoid mannequin
#       (Materials/M_Mannequin.demat) playing walk. Frame-to-frame motion is judged only at a real-time frame rate: the
#       Debug editor's --play renders a fixed 1/60 s per frame at <1 fps while the network moves on the wall clock.
